    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Build succeeds and the test passes. From the audit log you can see it working: a registration date of `2026-10-05` (October \u2192 fiscal year ending 2027) yields `membershipNumber=NSW-16E6609E-M27`, matching the new `FY27` fiscal year.\n\n## Summary\n\nMoved all date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts 1 July, named by the calendar year it ends in \u2014 so 2026\u201107\u201101 \u2192 FY27, 2026\u201106\u201130 \u2192 FY26).\n\nChanges:\n\n- **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a new read-only `fiscalYear` property (`FY<YY>`) to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it. Updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to reflect the fiscal-year basis.\n- **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`**:\n  - Added a `fiscalYear` mapping plus `formatFiscalYear(owner)` producing `FY<YY>` from the business-day-adjusted `registrationDate`.\n  - Added a shared `fiscalYearEndYear(date)` helper (month \u2265 July \u2192 year+1).\n  - Changed `formatMembershipNumber` so the `-M<YY>` segment uses the fiscal year instead of the raw registration-date year.\n  - Changed the tenure rule in `formatMembershipPoints` from a 365-day window to *elapsed fiscal years > 1*, and removed the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import.\n\nThe stored `registrationDate` is already business-day-adjusted (via the controller and the `@PrePersist` backstop), so all three derived values consume that adjusted date. Test suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
